 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Performance:** Significantly higher quality than single-stage uniform sampling, particularly for complex geometry and view-dependent effects, at the cost of roughly double the network queries per ray [S5, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Differentiable end-to-end, allowing optimization from images alone; handles view-dependent appearance via directional encoding; hierarchical sampling efficiently allocates samples to dense regions [S1][S5, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Hierarchical sampling uses the coarse model's density to guide finer sampling, reducing aliasing and improving detail compared to single-stage uniform sampling [S7, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Differentiable end-to-end, allowing optimization from images alone; handles view-dependent appearance via directional encoding; hierarchical sampling efficiently allocates samples to dense regions [S1][S7, unofficial].
 - **Limitations:** The basic implementation is inefficient and does not converge to a sufficiently high-resolution representation for complex scenes without hierarchical refinement [S1].
 
 ## Background
-NeRF (Neural Radiance Fields) was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1]. It represents a scene as a continuous 5D function mapping spatial coordinates and viewing directions to volume density and emitted color [S1]. The method is trained by minimizing the difference between rendered images and ground truth images using gradient descent, leveraging the differentiability of the volume rendering integral [S1]. The coarse-to-fine sampling strategy was introduced to address the inefficiency and aliasing artifacts of uniform sampling, allowing the model to focus computational resources on regions with high density [S5, unofficial].
+NeRF (Neural Radiance Fields) was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1]. It represents a scene as a continuous 5D function mapping spatial coordinates and viewing directions to volume density and emitted color [S1]. The method is trained by minimizing the difference between rendered images and ground truth images using gradient descent, leveraging the differentiability of the volume rendering integral [S1]. The coarse-to-fine sampling strategy was introduced to address the inefficiency and aliasing artifacts of uniform sampling, allowing the model to focus computational resources on regions with high density [S7, unofficial].
 
